    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a low-fat mayo prototype and the emulsion keeps breaking when we drop the pH below 3.8. can you explain what's happening at the molecular level with the lecithin specifically in high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the molecular level, lecithin (a phospholipid) relies on the electrostatic and hydrophobic balance between its polar head group and non-polar tail to stabilize oil droplets. As pH drops below 3.8, the high proton concentration protonates the charge on the phospholipid's phosphate group, neutralizing its negative charge and causing the charged head groups to repel too strongly for the viscous oil phase to maintain separation. Additionally, excess free fat globules in low-pH environments compete with lecithin for limited oil, physically displacing the stabilizing molecules and leading to emulsion collapse. To prevent breakage, your formulation may need more bile salts (like monocalcium phosphate) to maintain stability during acidification.",
      "source": "target",
