    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if the charge on the phosphate group is being neutralized, i'm guessing that's what makes the droplet coalescence happen? like, if they can't repel each other anymore they just clump? how much of the instability is due to that charge loss vs just the physical displacement i mentioned?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely correct; the neutralization of the phosphate charge eliminates the electrostatic repulsion between oil droplets, allowing them to approach closely enough for van der Waals forces to cause direct coalescence. While the loss of charge is the primary driver for this immediate breakdown, the physical displacement of lecithin by low-pH micelles acts as a secondary factor that accelerates the loss of stabilization. In high-acid environments, these protonated micelles actively chelate and remove free lecithin, creating a situation where both electrostatic failure and depletion of the emulsifier combine to destabilize the emulsion.",
